{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"antelopian-darkish-dormouse","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# `rebar comment <id> --body-file <path>` stores the literal string \\`--body-file\\` and exits 0\n\n## Observed twice, by two different sessions\n\n`rebar comment` takes its body POSITIONALLY (`Usage: rebar comment <ticket_id> <body>`). It has no\n`--body`/`--body-file` option. When invoked with one anyway, the flag token is accepted AS the body:\nthe stored comment is the 11-byte string \\`--body-file\\` (or the 6-byte \\`--body\\`), the intended\ncontent is silently discarded, and **the command exits 0** so nothing signals the loss.\n\nEvidence in the live store right now:\n- ticket b690 comments 5-9: five stubs of length 11, 11, 11, 11, 6 — an entire session's root-cause\n  analysis, fix rationale, blast-radius audit and live evidence, all lost.\n- session log 55a9 comment 5: also \\`--body-file\\`, written by an EARLIER session. So this is not a\n  one-off slip; the same footgun has now cost two independent sessions their durable record.\n\n## Why this is worse than a normal CLI usage error\n\nrebar's own guidance (AGENTS.md) is emphatic that the ticket store — not scratch notes — is the\ndurable, shared trail, and that agents should write progress and findings back as comments. This\nfailure mode silently defeats exactly that instruction, and it does so in the least detectable way:\n- exit code 0\n- no stderr\n- a comment DOES appear, so a \\`len(comments)\\` check (which is the natural way to confirm \"did my\n  write land?\") passes while the content is gone\n- the loss is only discovered later by something that READS the content — in this case the completion\n  verifier, which correctly failed a ticket for having \"no concrete attestation recorded\" because the\n  attestation genuinely was not there.\n\nThe cost is not the retyping. It is that work believed to be recorded is not, and downstream gates\nmake correct-but-confusing decisions on the missing evidence.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] a body argument that begins with \\`-\\` is REJECTED with a non-zero exit and a message naming the\n      correct form, rather than stored — unless the user clearly meant it (see below)\n- [ ] the escape hatch exists and is documented: a body that legitimately starts with \\`-\\` can still be\n      written (conventionally via \\`--\\`, e.g. \\`rebar comment <id> -- \"-starts with a dash\"\\`)\n- [ ] the same guard covers every positional-body command with this shape, not just \\`comment\\`\n      (audit at least \\`create\\`/\\`edit\\`/\\`session-log\\` for the same footgun and fix or document each)\n- [ ] a test asserts the REJECTION for \\`--body-file <path>\\` and \\`--body <text>\\` specifically, since\n      those are the two forms that have actually occurred\n- [ ] consider (and decide explicitly, either way) whether to ADD a real \\`--body-file\\` option: the\n      fact that two independent sessions reached for it suggests it is the expected interface for\n      long bodies, and shell quoting of multi-KB markdown through a positional arg is awkward\n- [ ] no silent truncation or coercion anywhere in the fix: a body that cannot be stored as given must\n      fail loudly\n\n## Notes\n\nFiled OUTSIDE epic 061c — unrelated to the LLM provider seam; this is a CLI/store-durability concern.\nFound when the completion verifier failed story b690 for missing attestations that had in fact been\nwritten five times over.","id":"00da-1bda-d59c-4c2e","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"rebar comment silently stores a flag-looking argument as the comment body, destroying the record (has now hit two sessions)"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785622876751905001,"uuid":"a32868cb-aa70-4d18-8118-baaf8ec52f93"}